Business Problem
Westpac’s payment controls had grown to 708 controls spread across four systems with no shared taxonomy. Nobody could answer a simple question: do we have the right controls, are any of them duplicates, and which one is actually the best? That answer existed somewhere in the data. Getting to it manually was not realistic.

The Data Analytics Solution
The first task was joining four disconnected systems (JUNO, Holocentric, ALEX, Alfabet) into a single analytical surface. From there, two assessment dimensions ran in parallel.

By product — across 75 product groups, GPT-5.4 compared controls and classified them as Redundant, Fragmented, a Consolidation Opportunity, or worth keeping as-is. The challenge was getting the model to compare the right controls against each other. We used high-dimensional text embeddings and cosine similarity to group semantically similar controls before the LLM ever saw them, so it was always comparing controls that actually had something in common.

By control objective — across 28 control objective groups (e.g. “payment error detection”, “data input validation”), the question shifted from classification to ranking. Which control, within a group, is the best designed? We used a two-pass LLM architecture: the first pass scored each control individually against expert-defined design criteria; the second pass received all those scores and produced a ranked view with a best-of-breed control identified separately for automated and manual populations. One important design decision: whether a control was Preventative or Detective was read directly from the source data rather than left to the model to guess.

Both dimensions used parallel execution, checkpointing, and retry logic to handle the scale reliably.

Benefits / Impacts

First structured, defensible view of control sufficiency across the payments book
Duplicate, fragmented, and redundant controls identified at a scale no manual process could match
Best-of-breed benchmarks established per control objective — something the business can act on
SME time freed from data gathering and redirected to decisions that matter

Adoption Barriers Addressed
The two hardest problems were trust and data.

On trust: LLM outputs were framed as advisory from day one. Two-pass architecture, SME validation gates, and confidence ratings meant reviewers could engage with findings rather than having to accept or reject them wholesale.

On data: nobody had ever joined these four systems at control level before. Building that linkage was as much of the work as the modelling itself.

Next Steps
To be confirmed once outcomes are shared. Likely candidates: expand beyond payments to the full active control population (~7,500 controls), operationalise duplicate control detection as a standing capability, and move the pipeline from a one-off exercise into something that runs as part of the regular control assurance cycle.
